  "summary": "A lubricating-oil console supplies clean, cool oil at controlled pressure to the bearings (and often seals) of large machines such as turbines, compressors and big pumps. Oil is drawn from a reservoir by a main pump; an identical standby pump starts automatically if header pressure falls. Duplex filters and twin coolers can be switched over while running for maintenance, and a pressure-control valve spills excess oil back to the tank. Positive-displacement screw or gear pumps are used because they give steady flow regardless of oil viscosity.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Package / system",
    "configuration": "Lube-oil console with main + standby rotary screw pumps",
    "application": "Turbomachinery bearing lubrication"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "pumps": "2 × 100 % three-screw pumps with motors (auto-start of standby on low pressure)",
    "filters": "Duplex with continuous-flow transfer valve",
    "coolers": "Twin shell-and-tube (water cooled)",
    "reservoir_note": "API 614 typically sizes reservoir retention time in minutes of normal flow — verify edition."
  },
